WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.440
Sky Shark.

00:00:01.740 --> 00:00:04.200
Nobody ever said it would be easy.

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:27.520
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:29.040
I'm Mike. I'm Sean.

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:33.960
And I'm Joe, and that back of the box changes my whole opinion on this game.

00:00:34.380 --> 00:00:35.800
No one ever said.

00:00:36.100 --> 00:00:37.120
Yeah, they're right.

00:00:37.280 --> 00:00:38.380
I should have thought of that.

00:00:39.500 --> 00:00:44.320
It sounds like something a critic would have said about the game, but no, they're upfront about it.

00:00:44.860 --> 00:00:47.380
Yeah, I guess I respect it though.

00:00:48.060 --> 00:00:48.960
Not that I didn't.

00:00:49.260 --> 00:00:56.660
Last week, I called this game Flying Shark at the end of the episode, which is fine because that is the arcade name.

00:00:56.660 --> 00:01:02.060
But the actual NES game that we're going to be playing is Sky Shark.

00:01:02.060 --> 00:01:05.440
Which leads me to my first question of the day regarding this game.

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:10.160
How much does the name of a video game matter to you now?

00:01:10.840 --> 00:01:17.940
Yeah, it matters when you're a kid and you're just shopping around and stuff, but do you ever not buy something because of the title?

00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:22.280
I don't know. Me personally, I could care less what the title of a game is.

00:01:22.280 --> 00:01:30.160
I mean, I think it's still a subconscious decision that you're going to make in your headspace, in your headbrain cavity.

00:01:30.380 --> 00:01:42.800
Like, if it's a kind of clunky sounding name, which I think Flying Shark kind of is compared to Sky Shark, you might think less of it or not give it the chance that you want.

00:01:42.800 --> 00:01:44.760
It's all marketing, Mike.

00:01:45.400 --> 00:01:55.100
Yeah, also, that's a hard question to answer, I feel like, in 2022, where it's like, I don't buy anything without researching it extensively beforehand, especially a video game.

00:01:55.180 --> 00:01:58.840
Like, I watch video, you know, I know what it is I'm buying and getting into it.

00:01:58.900 --> 00:02:05.760
So it's harder now than it was when I was younger, when I was, like, just looking at, like, on the shelf of, you know, like a GameStop, being like, that looks cool.

00:02:06.980 --> 00:02:08.100
Sean, you're right, though.

00:02:08.540 --> 00:02:19.620
Flying Shark doesn't adequately describe the, you know, the type of biplane that you're controlling in this game as Sky Shark does.

00:02:19.740 --> 00:02:27.000
Like, Sky Shark is more of a, oh, this is a fighter plane, whereas Flying Shark could be a game where you take Jaws and put them in the cloud.

00:02:28.640 --> 00:02:33.460
I didn't think of it in a more, like, literal sense, I guess.

00:02:33.460 --> 00:02:46.300
I just thought, you know, it's not quite alliteration, but Sky Shark kind of flies off the tongue a little bit more than Flying Shark, which just sounds like it's describing something.

00:02:48.360 --> 00:02:55.440
I'm just thinking about, like, the Legend of Zelda games that all of them, right, we don't call them Legend of Zelda 4, Legend of Zelda 5.

00:02:56.100 --> 00:03:00.420
Right. We don't call those games, you know, numbers or anything like that.

00:03:00.500 --> 00:03:09.560
They actually get, like, really nice poetic titles, you know, for some of them, and yet I'm not gonna, like, judge the game at all if it has, like, less of a title.

00:03:09.780 --> 00:03:13.140
You know, Breath of the Wild, Wind Waker, Phantom Hourglass.

00:03:13.360 --> 00:03:14.240
It's like, throw it at me.

00:03:14.760 --> 00:03:15.560
Zelda 2.

00:03:15.920 --> 00:03:19.360
Which is the only one that's, yeah, not the Legend of Zelda 2.

00:03:19.760 --> 00:03:22.820
True. Just Zelda 2, the Adventure of Link.

00:03:23.360 --> 00:03:26.180
Yeah. It wasn't afraid to, you know, to branch out a little bit.

00:03:26.180 --> 00:03:29.580
Anyway, we're gonna start talking about Sky Shark now, for real this time.

00:03:29.860 --> 00:03:31.520
And, uh, just a quick overview.

00:03:31.740 --> 00:03:33.180
Sky Shark is a shmup.

00:03:33.300 --> 00:03:35.080
It's one of those vertical shmups.

00:03:35.080 --> 00:03:38.420
Uh, very similar to 1942, 1943.

00:03:38.600 --> 00:03:46.480
All those, like, kind of, you know, games where it's, like, more in a World War setting or in a historical context style thing.

00:03:46.480 --> 00:03:54.060
Not, this isn't, um, filled with loads of cool power-ups that a biplane wouldn't have or, uh, a space setting.

00:03:54.260 --> 00:03:56.200
This is very grounded in our reality.

00:03:56.600 --> 00:04:03.320
Which leads me to my very first, uh, question about the game, which I thought I already said, but this is actually pertaining to the game.

00:04:03.320 --> 00:04:06.600
What is unique about this shmup?

00:04:06.760 --> 00:04:10.860
That's where I'm kind of at at this point in the, uh, chronological exploration.

00:04:10.860 --> 00:04:14.800
I'm wondering, every time we get a shmup, what's the reason for its existence?

00:04:15.020 --> 00:04:16.340
What makes it different?

00:04:16.800 --> 00:04:22.060
Here, I kind of want to lean on you guys a little bit to tell me what is different about this one.

00:04:22.340 --> 00:04:24.400
Yeah. Uh, and that's a great question, Mike.

00:04:28.480 --> 00:04:30.600
Thank you so much for answering, Sean.

00:04:30.840 --> 00:04:35.500
I agree. Uh, yeah, if you, if you twisted my arm, it would be tough.

00:04:35.660 --> 00:04:41.360
I mean, I guess the power-ups being so lackluster.

00:04:41.680 --> 00:04:43.100
Yeah, you already lost me.

00:04:43.220 --> 00:04:51.480
Is unique. Like, like, the power-ups being so simple and, like, not super interesting is unique-ish.

00:04:52.100 --> 00:04:54.300
Uh, maybe the difficulty.

00:04:55.320 --> 00:04:59.320
It's like, I think it's more difficult than even some of the harder shmups we've played.

00:04:59.500 --> 00:05:08.320
If you want my, my non-snarky answer, I think you, you kind of do have to get into the weeds, like Joe was sort of alluding to here.

00:05:08.660 --> 00:05:20.280
Um, I think the, the thing that sets this apart, um, than, than a lot of other shmups is that the projectiles, um, are faster.

00:05:20.280 --> 00:05:26.120
Um, that this is probably the fastest projectile, uh, shmup that I've ever played.

00:05:26.120 --> 00:05:38.520
Um, and that you can, at least in, in most regards, sort of see, uh, you could see the enemies tracking you instead of the projectile just sort of coming out of their body.

00:05:38.520 --> 00:05:41.760
Like, you can kind of juke them out a little bit.

00:05:41.760 --> 00:05:48.120
And really, I, I have to get that, that detail to, to point out anything different.

00:05:48.120 --> 00:06:00.020
Because we've seen, like, World War II, or I guess maybe Vietnam, in terms of, um, like, at least this, this era of, uh, like a setting for a game.

00:06:00.020 --> 00:06:01.540
We've seen that in shmups.

00:06:01.700 --> 00:06:03.460
We've seen the air stuff.

00:06:03.640 --> 00:06:05.020
We've seen land to ground.

00:06:05.220 --> 00:06:08.380
I'm sorry, uh, air to ground and ground to air enemies.

00:06:08.760 --> 00:06:10.160
We, we, we've seen everything.

00:06:10.160 --> 00:06:17.500
So you have to just go into the most minute little mechanics to actually have it set apart.

00:06:18.080 --> 00:06:27.660
Well, I think that's a great point though, Sean, because ultimately that's how I felt about this game was that it was a, um, the closest thing to an NES bullet hell.

00:06:27.660 --> 00:06:37.140
Both for the player and for the enemies, the projectiles are shot very fast by both leading to very intense reaction times on both the player and also the enemies.

00:06:37.240 --> 00:06:51.340
I think when you power up the thing that, yeah, it's like, it's a boring power up system, but ultimately it just continues to increase the number of projectiles you fire from a two spread to a three spread to, I think, as high as seven at one point.

00:06:51.340 --> 00:07:02.060
So ultimately this game is just about, uh, a, a plane in the air that has a large spray, but many, many enemies also shooting right back at it.

00:07:02.060 --> 00:07:18.740
And so this game is, for me was much more focused on where not to be and how to take care of the enemies that were coming on the screen and like learning their patterns than arguably any other shmup just because of how much it takes to survive in any of these levels.

00:07:18.920 --> 00:07:20.120
Not the, not just the later ones.

00:07:20.400 --> 00:07:21.480
Yeah. I'll say that.

00:07:21.600 --> 00:07:32.040
I think that, uh, you know, another thing that, that does make it unique, you know, started out sort of, we were, we were joking a little bit, but, but in all seriousness, the fact that, and, and all other.

00:07:32.040 --> 00:07:36.340
Other shmups. I'm usually because I'm not super focused on score.

00:07:36.780 --> 00:07:41.060
I'm usually like, I don't feel incentivized to just kill everything on screen.

00:07:41.240 --> 00:07:44.980
I'm like, I just want to kind of dodge things, get around, like kill things that I need to kill or whatever.

00:07:45.060 --> 00:07:46.240
But like, I'm, I'm trying to get through the level.

00:07:46.880 --> 00:07:52.980
This game incentivized me to kill everything on screen for no other reason than if I didn't, they would kill me.

00:07:53.760 --> 00:08:01.460
It like, I think that in this game is like the best example of, uh, like the best, um, the best defense is a good offense.

00:08:01.460 --> 00:08:09.080
You know, like the reverse where it's like, if I didn't kill everything, there were just in, in five seconds, there are going to be way too many projectiles on the screen for me to track.

00:08:09.580 --> 00:08:11.700
And, and, and I'll just have nowhere to go.

00:08:12.380 --> 00:08:20.460
Yeah. I think this is the first shmup, um, that I played that I was, and maybe I've just been playing shmups wrong this whole time.

00:08:20.460 --> 00:08:39.520
Um, where I was just sort of watching the immediate surroundings of my character and not so much what I was shooting at because I was trying to just like, uh, just get away from everything more so than, uh, destroy everything.

00:08:39.520 --> 00:08:44.500
Kind of, I guess like a little different than how Joe was playing because I couldn't focus on both.

00:08:44.660 --> 00:08:58.560
Like we'll get into it, but like there, sometimes there's so much stuff floating on the screen or the backgrounds as detailed as they are and, uh, mucking it up so much that I just can't, I can't see a lot of stuff that's coming for me.

00:08:58.560 --> 00:09:01.920
I don't have the mental bandwidth to track everything at once.

00:09:02.040 --> 00:09:05.540
So I just ended up having to like hone in on my guy.

00:09:05.720 --> 00:09:08.860
And you're fighting, you know, both land and air.

00:09:09.060 --> 00:09:23.620
So you have the fighter jets and larger planes in the sky to deal with, which for the first time ever, I feel like in a shmup, they're so aggressive that I can't tell if it's just their pattern design or if it actually is magnetized to the player.

00:09:23.620 --> 00:09:30.640
But some of these guys are like kamikaze style, like trying to bump into you while they're also shooting at you.

00:09:30.760 --> 00:09:37.400
So it's like one thing to dodge the bullets, but then you also have to be like, if I don't take this guy off the screen fast, he's just going to keep charging at me.

00:09:38.140 --> 00:09:42.920
Meanwhile, you have to deal with everything that's on land too, such as the tanks, the turrets.

00:09:43.180 --> 00:09:49.020
Um, I think, uh, you know, the battleships later on and stuff like that, that have multiple turrets on them.

00:09:49.020 --> 00:09:57.800
There's just a lot of enemies and different types of enemies to deal with all at once that can make for a very exciting experience.

00:09:58.000 --> 00:10:09.700
But I feel like the rate at which all of it is happening, even just from the beginning is, is too much for the type of, uh, you know, player you are, uh, not skill wise.

00:10:09.700 --> 00:10:21.780
I'm talking about like, you're not some awesome, futuristic, high tech space plane that can shoot like all different types of weaponry and switch on the fly between a large spray and a flamethrower and stuff like that.

00:10:21.960 --> 00:10:30.980
You're just a biplane that, you know, progressively shoots like one extra projectile that mind you, doesn't really increase the spray all that much.

00:10:30.980 --> 00:10:32.860
Uh, you know, the larger you get.

00:10:33.260 --> 00:10:39.400
The right type of shmup fan would like this game just for the pure crazy challenge of it.

00:10:39.740 --> 00:10:41.240
I'm not that type of shmup fan.

00:10:41.740 --> 00:10:44.460
I like, I like a good challenge in a video game.

00:10:45.680 --> 00:10:50.880
This didn't feel like a well enough, like intentionally designed challenge for me.

00:10:51.000 --> 00:10:52.980
It just felt challenging because there was just shit everywhere.

00:10:52.980 --> 00:10:54.220
Well, I don't know.

00:10:54.340 --> 00:10:57.880
Cause that's another point I was going to bring up that, that sort of sets this apart.

00:10:57.880 --> 00:11:21.540
Um, was that, um, that between the, the level like backgrounds, um, and the, uh, enemy patterns, like it seems to be like very, that they seem to be very handmade and, uh, actually sort of sculpted to match the context.

00:11:22.060 --> 00:11:23.100
Context of the scenery.

00:11:24.040 --> 00:11:32.180
Uh, like you, you'll be on a runway and then, uh, you'll see a bunch of tanks just sort of like line up down the runway.

00:11:32.200 --> 00:11:33.640
And like, that's the pattern.

00:11:33.640 --> 00:11:36.960
You kind of just get up in front of them and try and take them all out at once.

00:11:36.960 --> 00:11:38.860
Like it seems very handcrafted.

00:11:39.000 --> 00:11:47.040
Uh, it's just that most of the time I'm too panicked to actually like appreciate that.

00:11:47.500 --> 00:11:49.060
I don't know if that makes any sense.

00:11:49.060 --> 00:11:56.840
I agree that that, that, that does seem handcrafted, but the, the, the problem that, that comes up for me then is like, well, yeah, those things are intentional.

00:11:56.940 --> 00:12:09.340
But when each of those things are firing, no exaggeration, like eight to 10 bullets each, and I haven't taken them out quickly enough, there's a certain point where it's just, there's just a mess of bullets on the screen that don't feel intentional.

00:12:09.720 --> 00:12:19.360
And then I feel like, I mean, I guess if the, if the, if the, if the intention was to make me feel like I need to be aggressive and kill everything before they can shoot at me, then, then that was successful.

00:12:19.360 --> 00:12:31.180
I just don't know if it registered to me in the way that like, I like a challenging game, like, like a cuphead or, or, or even like, um, Elden Ring where it's like, you know, like those are challenges.

00:12:31.460 --> 00:12:35.100
Obviously it's hard to compare this because it's so different, but like, I can really recognize patterns.

00:12:35.280 --> 00:12:36.760
And they're fair. They feel fair.

00:12:36.940 --> 00:12:39.980
And in here, there was no pattern recognition for me.

00:12:39.980 --> 00:12:56.260
Yeah. I think that there's a, uh, for, for me that I couldn't feel the fairness in this game because a lot of the patterns, uh, look like they, they're put there to, to like corner you because you can only shoot in front of you.

00:12:56.400 --> 00:12:59.680
Um, like, you know, like a game like this, it makes sense.

00:12:59.820 --> 00:13:09.920
That's the way it works. But, uh, when you, when you end up sort of getting pushed to one corner, then you'll see another pattern come, which is shooting horizontally into that corner.

00:13:10.100 --> 00:13:12.720
And you have no way of getting out because of stuff shooting at you.

00:13:12.880 --> 00:13:15.460
Like it just, it's sort of triangulates on you.

00:13:15.500 --> 00:13:20.320
So I felt a lack of fairness in a lot of this game.

00:13:21.120 --> 00:13:29.400
Sure. And it's worth mentioning that the arcade version of this game, the flying shark, uh, which doesn't have the the in front of it, but I'm calling it that now.

00:13:29.400 --> 00:13:31.620
I'm changing the name of the title every five minutes.

00:13:31.760 --> 00:13:37.540
I've decided anyway, uh, it's worth mentioning that that is not like a one-to-one port here on the NES.

00:13:37.700 --> 00:13:48.300
They did rebuild the game and replan like where enemies like come from and how many enemies come from because in the arcade game, there's actually a bit of scroll to the screen.

00:13:48.300 --> 00:13:55.400
Something you don't usually see in a shmup, but you and the camera kind of move left to right, even though it's still a vertical shmup.

00:13:55.400 --> 00:14:01.160
So that some enemies are only seen if you're in some areas of the, of the map, if you will.

00:14:01.400 --> 00:14:06.000
And so there's a lot more of a lull between waves of enemies.

00:14:06.020 --> 00:14:11.340
And that feels more like the traditional, um, shmup that we're used to on this console.

00:14:11.340 --> 00:14:20.100
And I'm not sure if it was just like a different team from Taito was responsible for converting it over to the NES or what wound up happening here.

00:14:20.240 --> 00:14:27.560
But ultimately I think they took the game that they made had the, you know, the types of enemies that, you know, program the same kind of behavior.

00:14:27.560 --> 00:14:39.900
But then because there's no scrolling, because it's all fixed on one screen and because of the limitations of the NES, it just feels like there's more coming at you at once than was even true for the arcade game, which is funny.

00:14:39.940 --> 00:14:41.860
We don't usually experience that.

00:14:42.000 --> 00:14:45.580
Usually the arcade game is harder because they want you to put more quarters in.

00:14:45.580 --> 00:15:00.620
I mean, that's what I was going to say is that it's, it's interesting that you say that, uh, so much was changed between the two, because if anything, I feel like that this port, um, really captured the spirit of, of an arcade game.

00:15:00.780 --> 00:15:05.180
Because if an NES could take quarters, this would eat through all of them.

00:15:43.300 --> 00:15:45.560
So, I mentioned the power-up system.

00:15:45.560 --> 00:15:47.600
As pretty boring at one point.

00:15:47.860 --> 00:15:52.240
Uh, is that fair? Am I being, uh, like, am I just throwing that whole thing away?

00:15:52.360 --> 00:15:54.440
Should we talk about it a little bit more in depth?

00:15:54.800 --> 00:15:57.300
I think the power-ups themselves are kind of boring.

00:15:57.300 --> 00:16:02.440
Although I will say when you have all seven of those, it does feel like you've earned some power.

00:16:03.040 --> 00:16:10.400
Um, but, but like, just the fact that it's just like, you just get one more bullet in your spread each time is not exciting.

00:16:10.660 --> 00:16:13.180
You know, there's not like some crazy new bomb or something.

00:16:13.180 --> 00:16:18.400
But I do think what's, what is interesting is the, the, the, the means to get the power-ups.

00:16:18.660 --> 00:16:24.860
Where there's a, there's a formation of, I think, seven or six red planes that appear once in a while.

00:16:25.000 --> 00:16:28.540
And I don't think we've ever seen power-ups where it's like, you have to accomplish this task.

00:16:28.620 --> 00:16:29.800
It's not just shoot this thing.

00:16:29.960 --> 00:16:32.880
It's like, take out all seven of these planes when they appear.

00:16:33.020 --> 00:16:34.740
And they show up in different formations at different times.

00:16:35.160 --> 00:16:36.820
Did they do that? Yeah, they did that.

00:16:36.820 --> 00:16:42.480
And, and as red planes, in fact, not to, not to harsh or mellow here, Joe.

00:16:42.780 --> 00:16:43.660
No, it's all right. It's all right.

00:16:43.840 --> 00:16:49.440
No, I'm just saying it's like funny that if you look at this game, it's developed by a completely different company.

00:16:49.560 --> 00:16:51.400
1943 is Capcom.

00:16:51.400 --> 00:16:55.720
Um, I, you could argue they stole some of these assets, like the battleships and stuff like that.

00:16:55.880 --> 00:16:56.960
Look right out of 1943.

00:16:57.020 --> 00:17:03.180
Well, now it certainly seems like it, but I'll say that it, it was cool for 1943 to have come up with that.

00:17:03.300 --> 00:17:06.940
That's a cool, it's a cool, a cool point to 1943.

00:17:07.080 --> 00:17:15.760
And I know that it wasn't tied to any power-up, but I do know that in a lot of shmups, if you complete the pattern, you'll get like bonus points.

00:17:15.760 --> 00:17:20.140
Like there's at least some, uh, there's some reward for doing that.

00:17:20.320 --> 00:17:26.980
But yeah, I, I guess these two games are the only ones that we see a power-up, uh, come out of it.

00:17:27.280 --> 00:17:33.800
Yeah. And as someone who doesn't, who doesn't care about points, like I've never had, I never felt like I need to accomplish this little like side task before.

00:17:34.040 --> 00:17:43.580
But, well, I guess I did in 1943, but, but this time, yeah, it was, it was nice to have this like, this like little extra mission whenever those red planes came out.

00:17:43.580 --> 00:17:50.960
Like, I gotta get those because, because as much as the power-ups are boring, I do think that they actually are significantly helpful.

00:17:51.300 --> 00:18:02.160
Like, I actually feel like later in the game, only if I got power-ups and never died, which was very rare, but like would be easier because I had, you know, all those power-ups.

00:18:02.160 --> 00:18:14.720
Yeah. I mean, I remember I was, I was doing pretty well, um, and I was able to get, I mean, it wasn't a maxed out power-up tree or whatever, but I, I had a, a pretty thick shot.

00:18:15.560 --> 00:18:22.340
And, um, then, then I died, uh, it was, I think either late level two or in level three.

00:18:22.340 --> 00:18:26.380
And I, then I just, it was just a downward spiral and it was actually pathetic.

00:18:26.400 --> 00:18:30.420
Like, it was just a few seconds and then a death and then a few seconds and then a death.

00:18:30.640 --> 00:18:35.940
Like, like we've seen in, in, in many of these shmups, it's just a trope of the, of the genre.

00:18:36.300 --> 00:18:41.420
Like, once you die, it's just, it, it's a self-fulfilling thing where you're just going to keep dying.

00:18:42.140 --> 00:18:50.660
Yeah. Well, I was just gonna say, it feels also like this game, like nowadays, I feel like someone who's into like speed running or like a Nuzlocke challenge,

00:18:50.660 --> 00:18:58.920
someone who really wants to challenge themselves wouldn't, would, you know, would, would get more out of this game because it feels like you want to try and get through this whole game without dying.

00:18:59.220 --> 00:19:07.180
Because when you die, like for me, at least someone who's not good at this game, like if I die later in the game, like I might as well start over.

00:19:08.080 --> 00:19:14.100
Because like, I was like shooting that one shot does not really, uh, does not really, uh, give me enough of an advantage.

00:19:14.680 --> 00:19:20.520
But this kind of rapid reaction game, Joe, isn't the same as like a fulfilling challenge of a Nuzlocke or even like,

00:19:20.660 --> 00:19:30.860
people who play Kingdom Hearts 2 on critical mode or something like those games have like a systems thing that you're like trying to take advantage of and play it in a way that maybe the game didn't expect you to.

00:19:31.440 --> 00:19:36.920
